  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 124 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nNo major changes in the extended periods. For the first time in a\nwhile, we get to talk about the possibility of a small break in \nour weather pattern that brings some precipitation chances \ncreeping into the forecast (just in time for Halloween, of \ncourse).\n\nThe work-week continues to look sunny and mild with daily high \ntemperatures in the 70s and daily lows in the 40s and 50s. There \nwill be some variations of a few degrees thanks to some moving \npieces aloft on the periphery of the southeast, but the overall \nsensible weather won't feel much different day to day. Another \npotent trough diving across the northeast will bring yet another \n1035 surface high over the northern Appalachians, bring another \npsuedo-wedge airmass diving down the Appalachians and into the CWA. \nThis will reinforce the dry, Fall air in place on Wednesday and \ncould even keep highs closer to 70 or even the upper 60s in some \nplaces. Weak surface high pressure settles over the area Thursday \ninto Friday and will slowly warm a bit, bringing us into the mid to \nupper 70s (maybe touching a few 80s in central Georgia by Friday). \nThis airmass is dry and never really accesses any moisture thanks to \nbroad surface low that continues to churn just off the southeast \ncoast, so no precip chances expected.\n\nThis all may begin to change a bit going into Saturday. The players \nwill be another strong wave diving across the northern polar jet in \nthe northeast, building southwesterly flow aloft across the south, \nand a series of shortwaves embedded within that flow. \nUnsurprisingly, uncertainty in the forecast is somewhat high, as the \ntiming of each of these players may have big impacts on what we see. \nOf the ensemble members, the scenario which seems to deliver rain \nhas a faster timing of an initial shortwave within that southwest \nflow that runs into another potential wedge/CAD across us, even if \nweak. This allow for some additional overrunning of moisture and \nisentropic lift within the broader lift provided by the weak \nshortwave that generates some shower potential. As of right now, the \n15%-25% PoPs showing up Saturday afternoon into the evening seems in \nline with the number of models showing something like the above. The \nrest push better precip chances later due to short wave timing being \nslower, initial wave being even weaker, etc. Sadly, this means we\ncan no longer say Halloween will for sure be dry, and instead we \nwill need to watch things.",
